  "acl_vocabulary_note": "TWO SEPARATE VOCABULARIES. NEVER MERGE THEM. roles/acl (domain, over ad_acls) and roles/vulns/acls (host, over vulns_vars) each embed their own $aclExtendValues literal in their own PowerShell payload. They agree on the 19 standard rights and differ on the extended ones: the domain role added Ext-ManageCA and Ext-Write-SPN, the host role did not. Emitting Ext-ManageCA or Ext-Write-SPN into a host-level acls entry produces no ACE and no error — see acl_matching_note.",
  "acl_matching_note": "ORDINAL AND CASE-SENSITIVE. The check is PowerShell's `$aclValues.contains($right)` on a [string[]], i.e. Array/IList.Contains with the default ordinal comparer: 'genericall' does NOT match 'GenericAll'. An unrecognised right matches neither $aclValues nor $aclExtendValues, so $ace is never assigned, the `if ($ace)` block never runs, and the task exits having set $Ansible.Changed = $false. Ansible reports the task GREEN. A typo, a lowercase right, and a host-context Ext-ManageCA are therefore all indistinguishable from a correct idempotent run — validate the string BEFORE it ships, because nothing downstream will. Contrast $inheritance, which goes through a [System.DirectoryServices.ActiveDirectorySecurityInheritance] cast: that cast is case-INSENSITIVE and THROWS on an unknown value, so a bad inheritance fails loudly.",
  "acl_inheritance": {
    "values": [
      "None",
      "All",
      "Descendents",
      "SelfAndChildren",
      "Children"
    ],
    "observed_in_lab_data": [
      "All",
      "None"
    ],
    "note": "The full System.DirectoryServices.ActiveDirectorySecurityInheritance enum, which is what the cast accepts. Upstream lab data uses only All and None. 'Descendents' is Microsoft's spelling — not 'Descendants'; the cast is case-insensitive but not spelling-forgiving, and a wrong value throws rather than silently no-opping."
  },
